<p>German Chancellor Friedrich Merz said Friday in Wuerzburg that he would not currently advise his children or young Germans to live, study or work in the United States, citing what he called a rapidly changing social climate and difficult job prospects even for highly educated people. Speaking at a Catholic convention, Merz said he remained “a great admirer of America,” but added that his admiration “isn’t growing at the moment”. His remarks followed months of strain between Berlin and Washington under President Trump over trade, the wars in Ukraine and Iran, and NATO policy. Last month, Merz said the United States was being humiliated in the Iran war, angering Trump; days later, Washington announced a partial troop withdrawal from Germany and tariff hikes on European Union cars, a sector central to Germany’s economy.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/8V2WJ4KoQIskzsQyrw3V2dMeWFC7AOAvpVHOfu4uxlOK9FB10IyPoGZgsGsko3ntXMk4Qetiy99c65jmpdARXVnh2YY9ST5GQq1A686zgiWC' alt='German Chancellor Friedrich Merz attends a discussion with young people during a congress of the German Catholic people, Katholikentag, in Wuerzburg, Germany on May 15, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>German message: Merz told the Wuerzburg audience that Germany offered unusually strong opportunities for young people and urged listeners not to view world affairs only in “disaster mode”.</li><li>Troop move: A Hungarian report citing Politico said the Pentagon announced plans to withdraw 5,000 U.S. troops from Germany after Merz’s Iran remarks.</li><li>Audience reaction: Hungarian and Swedish reports said Merz’s U.S. comments drew applause at the Wuerzburg gathering, while the aside about his admiration for America drew laughter.</li><li>Domestic forum: At the same Catholic gathering, Merz acknowledged communication problems and said political disputes in Germany should yield results, not just argument.</li></ul><blockquote>I wouldn’t recommend that my children go to the US today, get their education there, and work there, simply because a social climate has suddenly developed there - Friedrich Merz</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Friedrich Merz: Merz rejected claims that his CDU party considered Germans lazy, saying he wanted to highlight Germany’s lower working hours compared with other countries and the need to preserve prosperity. (<a href='https://kurier.at/politik/ausland/friedrich-merz-usa-aufenthalt-abraten/403160007'>Kurier</a>)</li><li>Süddeutsche Zeitung: A commentary in the German newspaper questioned whether a chancellor should make such remarks casually and argued that Merz’s exchanges with Trump could not keep shifting unpredictably. (<a href='https://www.sueddeutsche.de/meinung/bundeskanzler-merz-usa-kommunikation-berlin-kommentar-li.3484019'>Süddeutsche Zeitung</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/2000579/wont-advise-my-children-to-live-in-us-says-merz'>Won’t ‘advise my children to live in US’, says Merz</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/germanys-friedrich-merz-says-wouldnt-advise-his-children-to-move-to-us-11500353#publisher=newsstand'>Germany&#x27;s Friedrich Merz Says Wouldn&#x27;t Advise &quot;His Children&quot; To Move To US</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/15/friedrich-merz-germany-children-study-work-us-politics'>German leader Merz says he ‘would not advise my children to go’ to US</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-chancellor-merz-says-he-wouldn-t-advise-his-kids-to-go-to-the-us/live-77168830'>Germany news: Chancellor Merz says he wouldn&#x27;t advise his kids to go to the US</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-05-15/germany-s-merz-says-he-wouldn-t-advise-his-children-work-in-us'>Germany’s Merz Says He Won’t Advise His Children Work in US</a> - bloomberg.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nation.com.pk/16-May-2026/germany-s-merz-says-w